Cohen Say Sports Teach Many Facts of Life By Johnny Hawkins
Society often labels athletes “jocks,” men playing boyish
games with their lives instead of facing the hard facts of life.
UT El Paso baseball coach Andy Cohen says, however, that
athletics can be a way of life, learning and wisdom.
“I've been into baseball on the professional level now for 45
years, and I've learned many of the basic necessities of life,”
Cohen said.

What makes the thud of a stick of wood against a speeding
sphere, the clasp of the ball in a glove and the race for that
diamond-shaped pot of gold enchant a man for his entire life?

“When I first played professional baseball in the late 1920s
and 1930s (Great Depression times), a player didn’t have a
managing agent, many benefits or much pay. It was a rough
way to make a living and you had to love the game to stay,”” Cohen said.

Cohen’s major league career briefly switched to minor league
status when he spent three years at Newark, N.J., then about
one year at Buifalo N.Y., followed by one more year with the
Giants. Later Cohen was traded to a Minneapolis team for
$25,000 and one man for his longest stint with a single team,
nine anda half years.

“Las always traveling during my career, which teaches me
50 many lessons about people und how to survive in the world
today,” Cohen said, “I remember watching my brother Syd of
the Washington Senators strike out“Babe” Ruth of the
Yankees in Ruth's last American League game.

“Ruth also hit a homer on Syd that day,” Cohen added.

One more year of ball playing with the New Orleans Pelicans
after the war boosted Cohen's carces as a player to 21 years.
“My most enterprising lesson in all those years was to learn
from sports to never give up at anything,” Cohen said.

Being appointed as last minute stand-in for the assistant
manager of the Philadelphia Phillis in a 1960 game against the
Milwaukee Braves was one of Cohen's most exciting bascball
moments. They won 5-4 in ten innings.

He has worked as a trainer, manager and scout for such major
league clubs as the Pittsburgh Pirates, Boston Braves, Brooklyn
Dodgers and Chicago White Sox.

Cohen has coached the UT El Paso team since 1962. Asa
basis for his team’s education he uses the “fundamentals” and
inside baseball tips taught to him by McGraw and the
philosophy of sports taught him by Luther Coblenty, his high
school coach.

Syd Cohen, the brother who fanned the Sultan of Swat, and
who also serves as an assistant to his brother, remembers when


‘blacks couldn’t participate in baseball with whitesso they had
